layout: post
title: "'Words' to 'Numbers' - A Tiny Conversion App"
description: A simple conversion app, that converts numbers written as words into numerical values. It's a Python program, where we use the inflect library to generate the words, and we use the word2number library to convert these words back into numerical values.
img: posts/numbers-to-words/code.jpg
author: Daniel Sauter
tags: Python, Programming, Conversion
---
In this post, I'll show you a small Python program that can convert a number from the written form to its numerical representation, and vice versa. We're going to use the `inflect` library to generate the written form of a number, and `word2number` library to convert this written form back into numerical value. Don't forget to install these libraries using pip: ```pip install inflect word2number```.

Here is the code:

```python
import inflect
import word2number


p = inflect.engine()


def number_to_words(n):
    return p.number_to_words(n)


def words_to_number(word):
    return word2number.w2n.word_to_num(word)

    
# Test the functions
print(number_to_words(143))
print(words_to_number('one hundred forty three'))
```

Just input the number in `number_to_words` function to get its written form and input the written form in `words_to_number` function to get its numerical value. 

Remember that the conversion from number to words only works for integers in the range from 0 to 999999999. For numbers beyond that range, the inflect library will generate a string with the number name instead of the number itself. If you need more advanced number to word conversion, you would have to write your own implementation. Similarly, for converting a number written as words to a numerical value, the library `word2number` can only convert the English language and some common misspellings. 

If you need conversion for other languages or custom cases, you would need to resort to Natural Language processing libraries or APIs. If you're dealing with very specific cases or a very large quantity of conversions, you might want to consider using a database to associate words with their values, especially if the values are not sequentially numbered words (like 'one', 'two', 'three'.... 'one hundred' etc). 

Don't forget to handle potential exceptions like those mentioned. As a good practice, always follow the philosophy "Failing fast" while handling such potential exceptions. As a final note, don't forget that a great percentage of mistakes in programming comes from trying to guess what a future developer might have meant, hence spending extra time on documentation of what you've created.

This program is a good starting point, it covers how numbers to words conversion works in Python. If there's any specific number to words or words to number conversion that you need help with, please don't hesitate to let me know. Happy coding!!!

