Please note that you typically do not actually run or manage the test cases. The purpose of these sample cases is to illustrate the way you can establish test conditions, evaluate methods, and validate the expected behavior of your code.

## Parameter Driven Tests
You can provide multiple data sets for testing a single unit test. This is commonly known as Parameterized Tests

For example:

```csharp
[TestFixture]
public class TestClass
{
    [TestCase(1, 1, 2)]
    [TestCase(2, 2, 4)]
    [TestCase(-1, 1, 0)]
    [TestCase(-10, -5, -15)]
    public void AddTest(int x, int y, int expectedResult)
    {
        int result = this.Math.Add(x, y);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedResult, result);
    }
}
```

In this example, the "AddTest" method is a parameterized test that takes a pair of integers and an expected sum as input. 

The TestCase attribute is used to indicate different test cases. Each test case is passed the input parameters to the "AddTest" method for testing. 

If the result of the method "Add" (which presumably is a part of the 'Math' class) is not the same as the expected result, a failure will occur.
 
You can write your test for such requirements using most of the testing tools which are available for .NET environment. Some popular ones include NUnit, MSTest, and xUnit.

As these things can vary depending on the test tooling you are using, please ensure you use the appropriate resources for your chosen tool.













 
 

